FMA4 / VPERMIL2 / XOP VPCMOV+VPPERM: VEX.W selects the wrong is4 / ModRM.rm operand roles

Every four-operand is4 instruction binds VEX.W the wrong way round in the opmap:
the W0 row is the "is4 third, rm fourth" form (…VIbW…) and the W1 row the "rm
third, is4 fourth" form (…WVIb). The AMD APM (Vol. 4, FMA4 and XOP encoding
tables, and every instruction page: "VFMADDPD xmm1, xmm2, xmm3/mem128, xmm4 —
… 0.src.0.01 69 /r /is4" vs "VFMADDPD xmm1, xmm2, xmm3, xmm4/mem128 — …
1.src.0.01 69 /r /is4") defines the opposite: W = 0 means ModRM.rm is the
THIRD operand (src2, the one that may be memory) and is4 the fourth; W = 1
means is4 is the third and ModRM.rm the fourth. LLVM and binutils encode and
decode it that way (validated against Bulldozer-era hardware).

For FMA4 the effect is a wrong answer for every encoding: dst = src1*src2 +
src3 becomes src1*src3 + src2 (vfmaddsd with 3, 5, 7 gives 26 instead of 22),
and the memory operand is folded into the wrong role. VPERMIL2PS/PD, VPCMOV and
VPPERM are swapped by the same table entries (their src2/src3 roles are
asymmetric too); they are corrected here under the same rule, but only the
FMA4 family has been diffed so far.

Fixed by exchanging the ATTR_VEX_W0 / ATTR_VEX_W1 attributes between the two
rows of each of the 24 groups (fetchdecode_opmap_avx.cc: VEX 0F3A 48/49,
5C-5F, 68-6F, 78-7F; fetchdecode_opmap_xop.cc: XOP8 A2, A3). Handlers and
.def rows are unchanged — the operand lists were right, only the W binding was
inverted.

Found 2026-09-14 by the hybrid-x86-oracle differential harness diffing the
zens model of the FMA4 group (every all-register vfmadd*/vfmsub*/vfnmadd*/
vfnmsub*/vfmaddsub*/vfmsubadd* form disagreed on the multiplicand/addend
roles). Present on upstream master as of 2026-09-14.

